<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/foundational/what-is-philosophy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Philosophy (Greek: <em>love of wisdom</em>) is the discipline of <strong>questions that every
    other field must assume answers to</strong>. What exists? What can be known? What is right?
    Its tools are argument, definition and careful distinction — and its history is a 2,500-year
    conversation where the same questions recur with sharper instruments.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the discipline mapped: areas, method, and its habit of spawning sciences" widget="equationStep" config={whatCfg} />

  <h2>Why study it (the honest case)</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>It trains precision:</strong> philosophy students score at the top on verbal reasoning and logic tests (GRE data) — the transferable skill is spotting bad arguments, including your own.</li>
    <li><strong>It clarifies the foundations of every other subject:</strong> what counts as evidence (science), what counts as fairness (law and economics), what counts as consciousness (neuroscience) — each field hits these walls eventually.</li>
    <li><strong>It preserves the unanswerable questions:</strong> not as failures but as frontiers — asking them precisely is progress even without resolution.</li>
    <li><strong>The practical branches have teeth:</strong> ethics of AI is shaping regulation; philosophy of science shaped the replication crisis response; political philosophy underlies every constitution (the ladder's lessons make each concrete).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Nagel, What Does It All Mean?; Russell, The Problems of Philosophy; Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy (plato.stanford.edu).</p>
</TopicPage>
